nal immediately.\n\nComparing the blocking options:\n- Playing at **E6** blocks the diagonal but is a passive move. It does not create any immediate threats for Black, allowing White to regroup or play I10 to force a response.\n- Playing at **I10** blocks White\'s diagonal win condition. Crucially, it also connects with Black\'s stones at I8 and I9 to form a vertical "Open 3" (I8-I9-I10). This forces White to defend against Black\'s threat in the next turn, shifting the initiative to Black.\n\nReason: The move at I10 is essential to prevent an immediate loss from White\'s potential Open 4. It is superior to other blocking moves because it simultaneously creates a lethal threat (Open 3) for Black, turning a defensive necessity into a counter-attack.\n\nAnswer: { "col": "I", "row": 10 }'}
